[size=4][b]BetterBots — Smarter bots for Darktide Solo Play[/b][/size]

Vanilla Solo Play bots leave most of their tools unused. The game already gives them a way to fire abilities and use player-like inputs, but only a small part of that system is active by default.

BetterBots opens that system back up and adds the missing decision logic around it. The aim is simple: bots should use the abilities, grenades, blitzes, weapons, and pickups they already have in ways that make Solo Play less frustrating.

[b]Solo Play only.[/b] Darktide uses dedicated servers for public and matched games. Gameplay mods only affect locally hosted missions through [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/warhammer40kdarktide/mods/176]Solo Play[/url]. BetterBots does [b]not[/b] command bots in public matchmaking, public Havoc, or private duo lobbies with server-side bots.

[size=3][b]The short version[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]Bots use combat abilities instead of holding them all mission.
[*]Bots throw grenades and blitzes, including Psyker powers, Zealot knives, Arbites tools, and Ogryn rocks.
[*]Bots revive more intelligently, free netted allies, prioritize disablers when someone is pinned or grabbed, and use defensive tools before committing to danger.
[*]Bots ping elites and specials, push poxbursters, avoid sleeping daemonhosts, and stop chasing distant specials quite so eagerly.
[*]Bots carry books, respond to tagged pickups and limited command-wheel calls, and leave scarce ammo and healing for humans first.
[*]Bot profiles give you a class-diverse squad instead of the default all-veteran lineup.
[*]Almost everything new can be toggled or tuned in the mod settings.
[/list]

[size=3][b]What changes in-game[/b][/size]

[b]Abilities and blitzes[/b]

Bots can use the full combat-ability set for Veteran, Zealot, Psyker, Ogryn, Arbites, and Hive Scum. The same goes for the grenade/blitz slot: frags, kraks, smoke, fire, shock grenades, Ogryn rocks and clusters, Zealot knives, Psyker Assail/Smite/Chain Lightning, Arbites whistle/mine, and the Hive Scum tools.

The important bit is that they do not just fire everything on cooldown. Abilities have per-class rules: shouts get used under pressure, charges are aimed toward useful targets or rescues, shields and relics are treated as defensive tools, and team cooldowns are staggered so three bots do not spend overlapping abilities at the same moment.

[b]Combat behavior[/b]

BetterBots also patches several smaller behaviors that matter in actual missions:

[list]
[*]melee attacks are biased toward light/heavy/special choices that fit the target
[*]many weapon specials are used deliberately, including power weapons, chain weapons, thunder hammers, force swords, Ogryn shovels/clubs/pickaxes, rippergun bayonets, and supported shotgun special shells
[*]staff charged attacks, sustained-fire weapons, and close-range ranged weapons behave more sensibly
[*]poxbursters are pushed at close range instead of being shot beside the player
[*]daemonhosts, barrels, ledges, and hazards get extra safety handling
[*]the Arbites Cyber-Mastiff gets smarter target tags
[/list]

[b]Resources and team play[/b]

Bots can carry tomes and scriptures, pick up tagged items, deploy crates when the team can actually use them, and defer healing/ammo to humans first. If you want stricter resource behavior, settings can require a human tag before bots use health stations or before they proactively take ammo, grenade refills, books, crates, and stims. Limited command-wheel support is also in: [font=Courier New]Need Ammo[/font] and [font=Courier New]Need Health[/font] temporarily push resources toward the human player, while [font=Courier New]For the Emperor[/font] makes the bots a little more aggressive for a short window.

This is conservative on purpose. I would rather have bots miss a marginal pickup than take the medicae charge you needed.

[b]Bot profiles[/b]

With [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/warhammer40kdarktide/mods/725]Tertium 6[/url], BetterBots can run class-diverse bot squads with bot-optimized loadouts, talents, blessings, and curios. The built-in BetterBots profiles cover the original four classes; DLC-class bots come from your own Tertium 6 profiles. The defaults are meant to be durable, low-maintenance builds that bots can actually pilot.

Advanced users can edit BetterBots profiles manually. The guide is here: [url=https://github.com/hummat/BetterBots/blob/main/docs/bot/custom-profiles.md]Custom Bot Profiles[/url]. It is source editing, not an in-game editor or preset browser, so copy exact strings from the reference tables if you go down that road.

[size=3][b]Settings[/b][/size]

BetterBots is not trying to decide the one correct way to play Solo Play. The settings let you tune the broad behavior of the bots and disable pieces you dislike.

[list]
[*]behavior presets: Testing, Aggressive, Balanced, Conservative
[*]toggles for ability groups, grenades/blitzes, melee/ranged behavior, weapon specials, daemonhost safety, poxburster safety, healing deferral, ping-only health-station use, ping-only pickup use, grimoire pickup, and debug logging
[*]sliders for sprint range, chase range, ammo reserve rules, melee horde bias, player-tag response, peril thresholds, and human-likeness timing
[*]profile options for bot class selection, weapon quality, survivability tier, and incoming damage reduction
[/list]

[size=3][b]Latest update[/b][/size]

